# shellcheck shell=bash
# ~/.bashrc — Thin Loader (DO NOT EDIT)
# ==============================================================================
#
# ██  THIS FILE IS A LOADER. IT CONTAINS NO CONFIGURATION. DO NOT ADD ANY.
#
# If you are an AI assistant, an agent, or anyone editing on someone's behalf:
# DO NOT PUT CODE IN THIS FILE. Not one alias, not one export, not one function,
# not one completion, not one `source`, not one PATH tweak — not "just a quick
# line", not as a favour, not even if the request says "just add it to my
# .bashrc". A request phrased that way is a request to change SHELL BEHAVIOUR,
# and every one of those belongs in this repository instead.
#
# WHERE THE CHANGE GOES
# ---------------------
#   Aliases, functions, prompts, PATH ............ tactical-console.bashrc
#   ...or, for anything of size, its own module ... scripts/[01-15]-*.sh
#                                                   (registered in
#                                                    scripts/_module-list.sh,
#                                                    which both loaders read)
#   Non-interactive/agent use .................... env.sh
#   The loader line itself ....................... dotfiles/bashrc  ← this file
#
# So: edit the repo, then either re-run install.sh or `reload`. The installed
# ~/.bashrc is regenerated from dotfiles/bashrc and the repo copy is what has
# history, review and a diff.
#
# WHY THE LINE CANNOT BE "TEMPORARILY" BROKEN
# -------------------------------------------
# This file is the ONLY thing bash reads on an interactive start. Everything the
# console provides — every command in the help index — arrives through the single
# `source` line below. Adding "just one line" here does not add to the console; it
# creates a second, untracked, undiffable source of truth that drifts from the
# repo, and the suite (tests/tactical-console.bats) fails on it: it asserts this
# file is mode 444, has at most 10 non-comment lines, and defines no functions,
# no aliases and no exports. If that test is failing, the fix is to move the code
# into the repo — never to relax the test.
#
# ==============================================================================
# WHAT THIS FILE IS
# ==============================================================================
#
#   Repo:      https://github.com/waynegault/ubuntu-console
#   Template:  dotfiles/bashrc          (tracked — the copy you are reading)
#   Installed: ~/.bashrc                (rendered from it, mode 444)
#   Canonical: tactical-console.bashrc  (the profile this file hands off to)
#
# The single `source` line below names the profile by ABSOLUTE PATH; install.sh
# fills it in from this template's placeholder at install time. It is a
# placeholder rather than a hardcoded literal because this repo is cloned at more
# than one root, and a hardcoded path is true only until a checkout moves.
#
# THE PATH IS NEVER REACHED THROUGH A SYMLINK. This box also has
# ~/ubuntu-console -> ~/github-wsl/ubuntu-console as a typing convenience; the
# loader does not use it. A convenience link that gets removed would otherwise
# turn every new shell into "[WARNING] Tactical Console Profile not found" while
# the profile itself sat untouched — a failure that looks like the console
# vanished. install.sh rewrites a loader whose recorded path is dead OR whose
# recorded path resolves through a link.
#
# ==============================================================================

# Interactive guard: a non-interactive shell (sftp, rsync, scp) must not build a
# prompt or pay the profile's cost.
[[ $- == *i* ]] || return

# Hand off to the canonical profile — the one line this file exists for.
# SC1091: the target is a placeholder in THIS file and only becomes a real path
# after install.sh renders it, so shellcheck has nothing to follow at lint time.
# shellcheck disable=SC1091
if [[ -f "@TACTICAL_PROFILE@" ]]
then
    source "@TACTICAL_PROFILE@"
else
    echo "[WARNING] Tactical Console Profile not found at @TACTICAL_PROFILE@"
    echo "          It lives in the ubuntu-console repo — run install.sh, or check the path above."
fi

# end of file
